          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "An integral condition becomes an algebraic equation after evaluating the upper and lower limits."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Make one coefficient agree",
      "body": "<p>Multiply one equation so that one parameter has the same coefficient in both equations, then subtract.</p>",
      "recap": "Elimination removes one unknown while preserving both equations.",
      "pitfall": "The two equations must use the same a and b.",
      "pause": "Solve for both parameters and calculate the requested sum.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-extra-simultaneous-equations",
          "reminder": "Elimination removes one unknown while preserving both equations."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Evaluate the first condition.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2. Evaluate the second condition.</strong> Expand the integrand: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3. Solve the equations together.</strong> Twice the second gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Subtract the first to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. <strong>Answer F.</strong></p>",
